      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 European Pain Federation - EFIC®",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The development of antinociceptive tolerance following repetitive administration of opioid analgesics significantly hinders their clinical use. Evidence has accumulated indicating that microglia within the spinal cord play a critical role in morphine tolerance. The present study investigated the effects and possible mechanisms of a natural compound, paeoniflorin, in morphine tolerance via its specific inhibition of microglial activation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The microglia cell line BV-2 was used. Cytokine expression was measured using quantitative polymerase chain reaction. Cell signalling was assayed by Western blot and immunohistochemistry. Nociception was assessed in Sprague-Dawley rats and CD-1 mice using Hargreaves' methods or the hot-plate test, respectively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "(1) Morphine induces robust BV-2 cell activation, as evidenced by increased p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK) phosphorylation, nuclear factor (NF)-κB translocation and proinflammatory cytokine expression. These changes are inhibited by paeoniflorin. (2) Co-administration of paeoniflorin with morphine potentiates morphine antinociception by inhibiting p38 MAPK/NF-κB signalling in the spinal cord. (3) Co-administration of paeoniflorin suppresses morphine-increased expression of toll-like receptor-4 both in BV-2 cells and within the spinal cord following chronic morphine treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Paeoniflorin directly suppresses morphine-induced microglial activation and thus results in potentiation of morphine acute analgesia and attenuation of morphine chronic antinociceptive tolerance."
          }
        ]
      },
